WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.389 --> 00:00:14.059
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:18.769
For a franchise so surrounded in mystery,
it seems fitting that nobody had a clue The

00:00:18.769 --> 00:00:23.050
Cloverfield Paradox had even been made, let
alone that is was being released within hours

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:24.640
of its announcement during the Superbowl.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.470
Now, there are plenty of reasons why the Cloverfield
Paradox should have worked and plenty of reasons

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:30.830
why it didn’t.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:34.551
But we’re going to focus on the element
that makes 2 largely unrelated scripts, so

00:00:34.551 --> 00:00:38.820
unique and cohesive, and makes the third one
such an epic fail.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:39.820
That element?

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:42.480
Our relationship to reality and trauma.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.600
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on The Cloverfield
Paradox: What Went Wrong?

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.609
And spoilers ahead for all the Cloverfield
movies.

00:00:49.609 --> 00:00:53.690
Before we dip into that secret sauce, a recap
of The Cloverfield Paradox is in order.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:57.250
The year is 2028, and the world is in the
midst of an energy crisis.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:02.570
The Crew aboard Cloverfield Station are orbiting
Earth to test the experimental Shepard particle

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:07.970
accelerator which, they hope, will save the
world by producing safe, clean energy.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:11.240
Just before they turn on the machine, though,
we overhear an interview with a writer who

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.900
thinks turning on the particle accelerator
will open a wormhole to other dimensions and

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.390
release God-knows-what onto an unsuspecting
population.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:25.070
"This experiment could unleash chaos, the
likes of which we have never seen!"

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:26.340
And surprise!

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.850
This is exactly what happens; they are teleported
into another dimension, across space where

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:36.520
they experience the combined horrors of phantom
limbs, creepy crawlers, and faulty wiring.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.860
The crew must figure out how to get home,
and well, you can probably figure out where

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:40.860
it goes from there.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.100
If there is one thing we should be excited
about getting in The Cloverfield Paradox - it’s

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.100
answers.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.130
What was the giant monster that attacked New
York at the end of the first one?

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:50.520
Did it survive the nuke?

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.490
If we hoped to see those answers in 10 Cloverfield
Lane, well, we’re out of luck.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:58.460
Instead, we got Walter Sobchak remindiing
us that this isnt 'Nam, there are rules.

00:01:58.460 --> 00:01:59.460
"Yeah, Michelle!

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:00.460
That's great.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:01.460
Except it was Emmet's turn.”

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.320
“Sorry, I just got a little excited.”

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:04.320
“Yeah, well.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.289
I'm keeping that point."

00:02:06.289 --> 00:02:11.400
And, as our protagonist escapes his clutches,
she enters a world of pain... and aliens.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.400
"Come ON."

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.570
But The Cloverfield Paradox DOES give us answers.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:16.570
Those giant monsters?

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:18.000
It’s from another dimension.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.400
The camcorder video?

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.790
It might not even be from the same timeline.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:25.150
So, why isn’t getting these answers more
rewarding?

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:29.200
Cloverfield, and 10 Cloverfield Lane, are
both films about trauma.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.680
Both the trauma one might associate with war,
natural disaster, or extra-dimensional aliens

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.150
destroying your home, and the trauma of being
physically trapped in a relationship with

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:40.209
a person that’s gonna go full Jack Torrance
at any minute.

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:41.410
“Here’s Johnny!”

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:46.230
10 Cloverfield Lane brings up all the same
aspects of abusive relationships: possible

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:49.000
gaslighting, "There is nowhere TO go, Michelle."

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.590
and threats of violence interspersed with
normal family fun.

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:56.700
But the first two movies aren’t just about
dealing with trauma: they’re about the impossibility

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.130
of fully comprehending that trauma.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:03.849
These insanely violent encounters with a lack
of closure speak to one of the fundamental

00:03:03.849 --> 00:03:06.250
difficulties of navigating the world.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:11.130
For philosopher and sometimes-kleptomaniac
Jacques Lacan, there exists a fundamental

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:17.370
human inability to fully grasp what he calls
“The Real,” or the world as it truly is.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.629
Cloverfield throws us in the midst of an alien
invasion that evades easy description.

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:25.810
We’re often given the byproduct of what’s
happening, or people’s reactions, but almost

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:29.379
never get the source of the turmoil in an
explainable way.

00:03:29.379 --> 00:03:33.060
Like when the head of the Statue of Liberty
comes rolling down the street or a horrifically

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.959
disfigured woman is begging for help.

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:37.360
“Let me in, let me IN!”

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:42.090
In the first Cloverfield, the reality of what’s
happening only sparsely comes in news segments

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:43.320
in the background.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:48.010
One of the ways Cloverfield achieves this
is with perspective; it’s filmed POV-style,

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:52.489
shot by the characters in the movie and begins
with a detailed look at our main cast of characters

00:03:52.489 --> 00:03:55.019
during a party for the protagonist, Rob.

00:03:55.019 --> 00:03:59.750
In the middle of the shindig, they’re interrupted
by an explosion signaling the monster invasion.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:03.900
Their battle for survival is intercut with
older video that is being recorded over, which

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.420
gives you glimpses into the relationship between
Rob and Beth.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:11.860
This subjective point of view means we only
ever get hints of where and what the monster

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:15.720
is, how the military are responding to it,
and even what it looks like.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.700
Y’know, just like you would in real life.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:21.580
By shooting it this way, we don’t know what
kind of monster they are facing but we do

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:25.229
know who the characters are and what they
are facing, personally.

00:04:25.229 --> 00:04:28.000
10 Cloverfield Lane employs a similar technique.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:32.349
It doesn’t use this same POV-style to cut
us off from the reality of what’s happening,

00:04:32.349 --> 00:04:35.710
but rather uses a concrete bunker to similar
ends.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:40.070
The protagonist Michelle wakes up in a bunker
that she can’t leave for reasons that seem

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:41.970
suspicious, to say the least.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:43.260
She’s told she was “rescued.”

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:47.580
“Given as how I saved your life, I think
that’s acceptable” and that the outside

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:48.580
world is deadly, “Everyone outside of here
is dead."

00:04:48.580 --> 00:04:55.470
Now, if we suspect that Howard is preparing
to wear Michelle’s skin, we get just enough

00:04:55.470 --> 00:05:00.599
interaction from the outside world to give
credence to his story, but not enough to truly

00:05:00.599 --> 00:05:02.080
know what’s happening.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.940
As the movie ends, we get enough information
to let us know that things aren’t going

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:08.270
great for Earth, but not much else.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:13.220
The control of information in both movies
is what makes them so tense and entertaining.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:17.289
We have to fill in the gaps, which instead
of being frustrating, makes for the sort of

00:05:17.289 --> 00:05:19.700
thing that spawns endless fan theories.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:24.820
For Lacan, that reality which seems to evade
us, “The Real,” is disconnected from our

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:26.729
own personal experience.

00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:30.950
Only through language, or what he calls the
“symbolic,” can we bridge the gap between

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.380
our minds and the reality beyond them.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:38.190
What Cloverfield explores, is the chasm between
“the real” and language, the inability

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:39.600
to bridge that gap.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:44.899
The characters and us, the audience, want
answers, we want to understand what is happening

00:05:44.899 --> 00:05:48.150
but the film withholds the language to explain
it.

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:52.450
That chasm, for Lacan, is called the ‘Lack,’
and it’s what drives the tension of the

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:53.800
first two movies.

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:57.490
We all know what it’s like to be at a “loss
for words” during a terrible event.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:02.200
Footage of 9/11 shows onlookers covering their
mouths in silent horror, while watching the

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.170
tragic event happen in front of them.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:09.530
It’s no secret that the cinema verite, found-footage
style of the original Cloverfield was inspired

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:12.010
by the events and coverage of 9/11.

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:17.280
That kind of cultural trauma runs deep and
was still a raw nerve at the time of the trailer’s

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.280
release.

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:21.770
Mass entertainment wasn’t willing to return
to these sorts of disaster movies right away

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:26.510
but the movie is able to tap into that that
post 9/11 anxiety by borrowing from the structure

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:31.339
of Kaiju movies, thereby using a symbol to
represent that shared trauma.

00:06:31.339 --> 00:06:36.330
This is what makes it so cathartic. 10 Cloverfield
Lane, released some 8 years later at a time

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:40.459
of cultural uncertainty and change, was a
paranoid thriller that makes you question

00:06:40.459 --> 00:06:41.900
the people around you.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:47.250
Against this gap between language and reality,
The Cloverfield Paradox gives us its antithesis:

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:48.250
science.

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:49.900
“This is the paradox.”

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:50.960
“Excuse me?”

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:53.230
“Particles interacting with each other across
two dimensions.”

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.280
“Okay, I’m gonna sit down.”

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.970
“Two distinct realities, in a multiverse.”

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:02.169
While there are certainly unexplained events
- like why the hell did Mundy’s arm keep

00:07:02.169 --> 00:07:03.630
moving after it was detached?

00:07:03.630 --> 00:07:08.409
The whole premise of the movie, trans dimensional
travel, explains the origin of the Cloverfield

00:07:08.409 --> 00:07:11.469
‘event’ in the first fifteen minutes of
the movie.

00:07:11.469 --> 00:07:16.760
“Monsters, demons, beasts from the sea—“
“To clarify, you believe their efforts to

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:19.589
solve the energy crisis might unleash demons?”

00:07:19.589 --> 00:07:20.589
“Yeah.

00:07:20.589 --> 00:07:21.589
And not just here and now.

00:07:21.589 --> 00:07:24.710
In the past, in the future.

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:26.080
In other dimensions.”

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.440
And sure, we’re not saying mysteries can’t
be solved, or that science can’t do it,

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:33.930
but it shouldn’t be as background noise
in the first half of the movie.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:38.660
Turns out the terrifying, ineffable monstrosities
that have invaded Earth for reasons beyond

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:41.520
our imagining happened because Science.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:42.640
Little disappointing isn’t it?

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:46.250
And if you’re going to give an explanation
or revelation, there’s a certain way to

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:47.250
do it.

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:50.810
Good examples tend to give us a paradigm shift
or clarify motives.

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:55.270
In exceptional cases, they can give you an
entirely new way to view the events prior,

00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:58.630
like when we find the truth about Tyler Durden
or the Man in Black.

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:03.360
How does quantum entanglement add to, or reframe
the original Cloverfield stories?

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:04.360
It doesn’t.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:08.470
And besides breaking away from the kind of
trauma that defined the first two movies,

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:13.060
there is no central fear or shared trauma
for the audience to latch on to.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:17.919
If Cloverfield was the fear of an invasion,
and 10 Cloverfield Lane was the fear of other

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:20.950
people, what’s the central fear of this
movie?

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:22.250
Fear of Particle Accelerators?

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:23.490
Peak oil?

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:28.279
By explaining away the mystery almost as an
afterthought, you rob the audience not just

00:08:28.279 --> 00:08:30.419
of their mystery but their catharsis.

00:08:30.419 --> 00:08:35.430
While, yes, “Explaining a mystery ruins
the fun” is a kinda obvious point, it’s

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:39.640
what creates our desire for mystery that is
important to the Cloverfield movies.

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:44.720
Lacan’s idea of Lack gets to the heart of
understanding why we love a good mystery and

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:49.700
why JJ Abrams’ properties seem to have such
a hard time with resolving those mysteries.

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:51.730
We’re looking at you, Lost.

00:08:51.730 --> 00:08:56.060
Just as the reality around us always evades
easy answers and the language to describe

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.840
it, we don’t want everything explained.

00:08:58.840 --> 00:09:03.200
Not just because we all like a good conspiracy
theory but because of the catharsis that comes

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:08.010
with the shared experience of realizing that
some things can’t, and sometimes shouldn’t,

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:09.610
be put into words.

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:13.680
The first two Cloverfield movies knew this
and played on that which is what made them

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:17.000
so interesting and, ultimately, rewarding.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:24.050
The Cloverfield Paradox, paradoxically, ruins
the fun by giving us all the answers and pretty

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:25.700
half-baked ones at that.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:27.370
So, what do you think, Wisecrack?

00:09:27.370 --> 00:09:30.210
Can the movie have more tastefully explained
the mystery?

00:09:30.210 --> 00:10:05.960
Let us know in the comments, and thanks 

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:19.030
for watching.

00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:31.700
Peace.

